                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "daf31486-e818-31a9-97a8-eab1abefae18",
                        "entry_id": "17bbda97-5b7b-3a12-9484-c18ce6ceff98",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Tod durch Überarbeitung und berufsbedingten Stress, zum Beispiel durch stressverursachten Herzinfarkt oder Schlaganfall oder auch Selbstmord"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Soziologie"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3b8424ce-8e29-43f6-9903-b430ef8e06e0",
                                "sense_id": "daf31486-e818-31a9-97a8-eab1abefae18",
                                "text": "Karoshi ist in Japan ein anerkanntes Phänomen."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e39a1bb3-c0c1-40c8-bf15-a4148f57290d",
                        "source_entry_id": "17bbda97-5b7b-3a12-9484-c18ce6ceff98",
                        "source_sense_id": "daf31486-e818-31a9-97a8-eab1abefae18",
                        "target_lang_word": "karōshi",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "karōshi",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6c3f7cf9-ecfd-3ed7-a05f-592fffae94d9",
                                        "entry_id": "496684bb-9f0e-3482-9162-e4f18d773e15",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Death, such as from heart attack or stroke, brought on by overwork or job-related stress."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "77d9ddbb-c893-46e5-b385-76e66815f88e",
                                                "sense_id": "6c3f7cf9-ecfd-3ed7-a05f-592fffae94d9",
                                                "text": "For a while he began to speak Japanese, rather slangy, never having seemed to learn it — karoshi for death from overwork, yakitaori-ya for eatery, and gaijin for clumsy foreigner."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "11ff28ac-a6fc-40ff-b6d6-07fe58522902",
                                                "sense_id": "6c3f7cf9-ecfd-3ed7-a05f-592fffae94d9",
                                                "text": "Second, we discuss the problem of karoshi, which is unique to Japan. Karoshi has become an increasingly serious problem."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ffce1090-0998-4cca-b1c3-ce5252ad1e72",
                                                "sense_id": "6c3f7cf9-ecfd-3ed7-a05f-592fffae94d9",
                                                "text": "[…] I am a workaholic. […] The Japanese have a word for the problem: karōshi. It means “death from overwork.”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5f5646dd-d8d4-4c95-980f-17ac45f3a20d",
                                                "sense_id": "6c3f7cf9-ecfd-3ed7-a05f-592fffae94d9",
                                                "text": "In Japan there is a word for the consequences of extreme busyness: karoshi''', which means 'death from overwork'. Karoshi happens when chronic fatigue, stemming from long hours and persistent stress, leads to stroke and heart disease. For most of us, the results of a busyness-induced allostatic load are much less dramatic, but still pretty bad: being 'always on' has been linked to reductions in performance, reduced memory, and increased health risks of all kinds: cardiovascular disease, reduced immune system performance and an earlier death. One expert in the US approximated that 60-90 per cent of all visits to the doctor were stress related."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4c4fbc13-98e3-412b-b1b6-1f4ce1dad991",
                                                "sense_id": "6c3f7cf9-ecfd-3ed7-a05f-592fffae94d9",
                                                "text": "Since the 1980s, labor lawyers and citizens groups have been pushing for changes to the law to recognize karoshi as a serious social issue."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
